WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.660
Good Morning, John

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.520
So as you may have noticed, I like to do all the jobs

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.600
VidCon is coming up at the end of this month, Lizzie Bennet just passed 50 thousand subscribers

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.200
SciShow and Crash Course and Truth or Fail and Ecogeek and DFTBA records; it's a long list

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.720
And sometimes people ask me

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.480
"Hank, how do you do so much stuff? You must have some excellent productivity tips"

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.840
And yes, I do!

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.220
Unlike apparently everyone else I put my pants on two legs at a time

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.500
I've developed a faster way of tying my shoes

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.920
And also a faster and more efficient way to ambulate

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.300
I predict within the next five years everyone will be walking like this.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.480
People also spend a HUGE amount of time eating, and I stopped doing that and instead

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.840
I just ingest 2000 calories of muscle milk in one sitting

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.220
This has also dramatically decreased the amount of time I spend pooping

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.120
though it has really increased the amount of time I spend peeing, which is why I've developed

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.460
this special desk chair

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.020
"Did you see that email from..."

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.680
What's up?

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.400
Okay...

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.120
Another tip, I my medical procedures in bulk

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.500
Also, I get my computer to do work for me

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.520
for example, I wrote a script that goes to Amazon for me so I don't have to do it, and orders more Muscle Milk

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.120
cause otherwise I'd spend a huge amount of time just ordering Muscle Milk

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.340
And I also wrote a script that emails my wife romantic messages on important holidays.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.000
I mean, I was really busy so I didn't write the whole program.  It was based on a Russian spam writing algorithm

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Here's any example of one from my last anniversary

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.780
But my biggest tip for all of you is instead of actually doing things

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.060
just imagine doing fun things

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:52.820
that way, you can get the maximum of stuff done, while in the end making sure that you never really ever do anything

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.920
Yes!  Kidding! I don't like Muscle Milk!

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.380
God, Michael drinks that stuff, it's so horrible.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:05.360
But for serious, I do do a lot of stuff and I am pretty serious about, you know, making sure I use my time productively

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.380
I use a thing on my computer called Rescue Time, that like tracks where and what I do while I'm on my computer

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.120
and it's great! It's sort of hilarious cause it's always like

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.860
"you spend too much time on YouTube!" and I'm like yeah, but it's my job Rescue Time!

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:26.260
But that leads to another point about how I have the awesomest job ever. I work a lot because work is more like a hobby for me.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:32.060
So that's where a lot of this like "all the jobs! do lots of different things" comes from for me.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.640
Like I just like making things.  It's one of the things that makes me excited about life!

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.000
I also have to say, that you know, I can be more productive because I have help!  Vidcon has a whole team,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.120
Carver and Monica and Jenny and Lindsey.  DFTBA records has Alan and Sam and Kristen.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:51.020
And then with SciShow and Crash Course, we've got a whole team.  I'd love to introduce you to them sometime, everybody, they're great.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.740
But I think that's one of the things that I learned from this project with you, John

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.960
is that I like to collaborate with people.  And no, none of them are as good of a collaborator as you are

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.780
you're still number one, but I like to do projects

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.840
I like to collaborate and I like to be on a team. It's like sports, except you're actually doing something!

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.560
And let me be the first to say what works for me in terms of productivity is not going to work for everybody

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:18.140
I don't like rigid, micromanaged schedules.  I like to let my, sort of, like internal stress level decide what I'm going to work on that day.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.400
If I get really busy and I freak out, just, you know, flip over Emma Stone's eyeball

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.320
And the funny thing is flipping over Emma Stone's eyeball, for me, is work!

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.340
So, thanks to everybody.  John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

